VCE.stimuli('psychology', {
  'e1-climb': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 1–4.</em></p><p>Tariq is rock climbing at an indoor gym. As he reaches for a hold, his fingertips brush a sharp edge and he instantly pulls his hand away. A second later he feels a stinging pain. He looks at his fingers, decides they are fine, and chooses to dip his hand in his chalk bag before reaching again. Moments later his foot slips and, for a few seconds, his heart pounds and he breathes rapidly.</p>` },
  'e1-drug': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 5–7.</em></p><p>Researchers are testing a new medication, Drug K. Drug K has a molecular shape that allows it to bind to the receptor sites that GABA normally binds to, and it produces the same effect on the post-synaptic neuron that GABA does.</p>` },
  'e1-piano': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 8 and 9.</em></p><p>Lucia has played piano for years using an incorrect fingering for a difficult passage. Her new teacher shows her a better fingering, and she practises it slowly every day for three months. By the end, the new fingering feels automatic and she rarely slips back into the old one.</p>` },
  'e1-amira': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 10–13.</em></p><p>Amira is in Year 12. For the last six weeks she has been studying late every night for her exams while also working weekend shifts. In the first week she felt energised and focused. She has recently caught her third cold in a month and says she feels "completely flat".</p>` },
  'e1-mice': {
    html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 16–18.</em></p><p>Researchers investigated whether a probiotic (beneficial gut bacteria) affects anxiety-like behaviour in mice. Forty mice were randomly allocated to four groups of ten. Two groups had their vagus nerve surgically cut (vagotomy); two groups had an intact vagus nerve. Within each pair, one group received the probiotic in their water for four weeks and the other received plain water. Anxiety-like behaviour was measured as the percentage of time each mouse spent hiding in the dark section of a test box.</p>`,
    chart: { type: 'bar', title: 'Mean % of time spent hiding', xLabel: 'Vagus nerve', yLabel: '% time hiding', categories: ['Intact', 'Cut (vagotomy)'], yMax: 80, yStep: 20, series: [{ name: 'Plain water', values: [64, 62] }, { name: 'Probiotic', values: [38, 61] }] },
  },
  'e1-puppy': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 19–22.</em></p><p>Noah is training his puppy, Biscuit. First, he clicks a hand-held clicker and immediately gives Biscuit a treat, many times over several days. Soon Biscuit's ears prick up and he wags his tail excitedly whenever he hears the click, even when no treat follows. Noah then says "sit"; when Biscuit sits, Noah clicks and gives a treat. Biscuit now sits quickly whenever Noah says "sit".</p>` },
  'e1-memory': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 27 and 28.</em></p><p>Mr Davies, a retired carpenter, suffered damage to one area of his brain after a stroke. He can still describe his favourite holiday in detail and name every tool in his old workshop. However, his physiotherapist notices that despite weeks of practice he cannot learn the smooth, precisely timed hand movements needed for a new hand-strengthening exercise, and his movements remain jerky.</p>` },
  'e1-sleeplab': {
    html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 32 and 33.</em></p><p>A sleep laboratory recorded the following for a participant during four separate 30-second periods of one night.</p>
<table><tr><th>Period</th><th>EEG (brain waves)</th><th>EOG</th><th>EMG</th></tr>
<tr><td>W</td><td>High frequency, low amplitude</td><td>Bursts of rapid activity</td><td>Very low</td></tr>
<tr><td>X</td><td>Low frequency, high amplitude</td><td>Very low</td><td>Low–moderate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Y</td><td>Slowing, with occasional sudden bursts (spindles)</td><td>Very low</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Z</td><td>High frequency, low amplitude</td><td>Some activity</td><td>High</td></tr></table>`,
  },
  'e1-mnemonic': {
    html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Question 3.</em></p><p>A psychology student, Jun, investigated whether using an acrostic improves recall. Sixty Year 10 students were randomly allocated to one of two groups. Both groups were given two minutes to learn the same list of 12 unrelated words, presented in the same order. Group A was given an acrostic sentence made from the first letters of the 12 words; Group B was given no strategy. After a 10-minute filler task, all participants wrote down as many words as they could remember.</p>
<table><tr><th>Group</th><th>Mean words recalled (out of 12)</th><th>Standard deviation</th></tr><tr><td>A: acrostic</td><td>9.1</td><td>1.2</td></tr><tr><td>B: no strategy</td><td>6.4</td><td>2.9</td></tr></table>`,
  },
  'e1-nurse': { html: `<p>Priya is a hospital nurse on a rotating roster: one week of morning shifts (7 am – 3 pm), then a week of night shifts (10 pm – 7 am), then a week of afternoon shifts. On night-shift weeks she sleeps from about 8 am to 1 pm, often scrolling on her phone in bed first. Her bedroom faces the morning sun and gets warm by late morning. She drinks two large coffees during each night shift, the second at around 5 am.</p>` },
  'e1-trial': { html: `<p>Two Year 12 students, Hana and Will, are both offered a place in the state cricket squad. Accepting means training three evenings a week and travelling interstate twice during the exam period.</p><p>Hana says: "This is my chance to show what I can do. My coach and parents will help me plan my study around it."</p><p>Will says: "I'll fall behind at school and let everyone down. I don't know who could help me manage it all."</p>` },
  'e1-needle': { html: `<p>Ella is 15. When she was six, she had a painful injection and fainted, waking up frightened on the floor of the clinic. Since then, the sight of a needle makes her heart race and she feels faint. She has missed her last two vaccinations because she refused to get out of the car. Each time, she felt immediate relief as the family drove away. Ella now says, "If I get a needle I'll faint, hit my head and end up in hospital." Her parents usually reschedule appointments when she becomes upset.</p>` },
  'e1-community': { html: `<p>A remote Aboriginal community established its own community-controlled health and youth service, governed by local Elders and staffed partly by community members. The service runs regular on-Country camps where young people learn language, songs and land management from Elders, alongside sessions on sleep, food and mental wellbeing.</p>` },
  'e1-er': {
    html: `<p>The following article appeared on a lifestyle website.</p>
<blockquote><strong>"Gut bugs beat exam stress!"</strong><br>A new study proves that eating probiotic yoghurt every day cures exam stress. Researchers gave 30 university students either a daily probiotic yoghurt (15 students) or an ordinary yoghurt that looked and tasted the same (15 students) for the four weeks leading up to their exams. Saliva cortisol levels were measured each week, and students rated their stress from 1 (very low) to 10 (very high). "I felt so much calmer," one student said. The study was funded by the company that makes the probiotic yoghurt.</blockquote>
<p>Summary of the study's results:</p>`,
    chart: { type: 'line', title: 'Mean saliva cortisol (nmol/L) in the weeks before exams', xLabel: 'Week of study', yLabel: 'Cortisol (nmol/L)', categories: ['Week 1', 'Week 2', 'Week 3', 'Week 4 (exam week)'], yMax: 20, yStep: 5, series: [{ name: 'Probiotic yoghurt', values: [9.8, 10.6, 11.9, 13.1] }, { name: 'Ordinary yoghurt', values: [10.1, 11.8, 14.2, 16.4] }] },
    source: 'Mean self-rated stress in exam week: probiotic group 6.9 (SD 2.4); ordinary yoghurt group 7.4 (SD 2.6). Hypothetical data created for this practice exam.',
  },
});

