    model: `<p>The gut–brain axis (GBA) is a bidirectional communication network between the gastrointestinal tract and the brain, linking the enteric nervous system to the central nervous system mainly via the vagus nerve. Gut microbiota can produce neurochemicals such as GABA and serotonin, so changing gut bacteria with a probiotic could plausibly influence psychological processes such as the stress response. Cortisol is a hormone released during stress that stays elevated during chronic stress, so saliva cortisol is a suitable objective physiological measure of stress in the weeks before exams.</p>
<p>The graph shows cortisol rose in both groups as exams approached, but it rose less in the probiotic group: by exam week it was 13.1 nmol/L compared with 16.4 nmol/L in the ordinary yoghurt group. This is consistent with the probiotic reducing the physiological stress response, possibly through gut–brain communication. However, self-rated stress in exam week was only slightly lower in the probiotic group (6.9 vs 7.4), and the large standard deviations (2.4 and 2.6) show the two groups overlapped considerably. Students in both groups still felt quite stressed.</p>
<p>The study has some strengths: the control group received a yoghurt that looked and tasted identical, so participants could not tell which group they were in, and cortisol was measured objectively each week. But there are important limitations. The sample was very small (15 per group) and included only university students, so the results may not generalise to other people, such as Year 12 students, which limits external validity. The study was funded by the yoghurt manufacturer, which raises concerns about integrity and potential bias in how the results were reported. The student's comment, "I felt so much calmer", is an anecdote, not scientific evidence.</p>
<p>Overall, the evidence offers only limited support for the idea that probiotics may reduce the physiological stress response. It does not support the claim that probiotic yoghurt "cures" exam stress: stress still increased in both groups, self-reported stress barely differed, and a single small, industry-funded study cannot "prove" anything. The study would need to be repeated with larger, more diverse samples by independent researchers before stronger conclusions could be drawn.</p>`,
